/**
 * The AGOS opcode argument tables — how long every instruction is.
 *
 * GENERATED. Do not edit by hand; regenerate with
 * `npm run gen:agos-tables -- /path/to/scummvm`, which reformats it afterwards.
 *
 * An AGOS instruction does not say how long it is. Its operand widths come from
 * a table outside the bytecode, and unlike AGI's — which ships with the game in
 * `agidata.ovl` — this one shipped inside Adventure Soft's bundled executable.
 * So the table is *ours* (ADR 0029), transcribed from ScummVM's
 * `engines/agos/subroutine.cpp`, which is this project's stated reference for
 * how the originals behave.
 *
 * It is generated rather than typed out because a single wrong character in
 * 2300 entries misreads every instruction boundary after it, re-emits that
 * misreading byte for byte, and passes a byte-identity check while the
 * structure it produced is wrong. That is the exact failure ADR 0013 named for
 * AGI and ADR 0029 inherited; a hand-copy is the likeliest way to cause it.
 *
 * Each entry is the operand string for one opcode. A space ends it. The letters
 * are decoded by `readInstruction` in `subroutines.ts`:
 *
 * - `F N S a n p v 3` — a 16-bit word
 * - `B` — a byte, or 0xFF followed by a byte naming a variable; a 16-bit word
 *   in Elvira 1
 * - `I` — an item id: a 16-bit word, with 1/3/5/7/9 standing for the special
 *   items and anything else followed by a 32-bit id
 * - `T` — a string id: a 16-bit word, with 0 and 3 standing alone and anything
 *   else followed by a 32-bit id
 *
 * A `null` entry is an opcode the game never uses and the interpreter has no
 * length for. Reaching one is a decode failure, not a no-op.
 */

